Target: Meizu 21 Note, 5.15.119-android13-8-g6ff5097ee32a-ab1764665171 (qcom,kalama).
That kernel has CONFIG_IP_SET=n, so neither the ipset core, any set type, nor the
xt_set match exists. All of them are ordinary mainline modules.

Adaptations: none. Every file is byte-identical to the pinned source. Each module
keeps its upstream module_init/module_exit and its MODULE_ALIAS entries, and they
are built under prefixed names (droidspaces_ip_set*, droidspaces_xt_set) so they
cannot collide with a native ipset build.

Build flags: -DCONFIG_IP6_NF_IPTABLES=1 matches the device, which sets that option,
and keeps ip_set_getport() able to parse IPv6 transport ports. CONFIG_BRIDGE_NETFILTER
is deliberately not defined because the device does not set it; ip_set_hash_netiface
then builds the non-bridge variant, matching host behaviour.

Loading: the kernel resolves module-to-module imports at insmod time, so the loader
must load droidspaces_ip_set first, then the set types, then droidspaces_xt_set.
modules.json lists that order, and droidspaces_ip_set exposes no module parameters;
readiness is checked through /sys/module/<name>.

Userspace: no binary is shipped. The `ipset` command from a distribution (for
example Debian's ipset package inside a Droidspaces container, or the Android host
binary some third-party modules ship) speaks the native nfnetlink protocol to the
registered core. Nothing in the module depends on a particular userspace build.

Cross-check: the third-party IPSET_LKM v2.5 android13-5.15 release builds the same
upstream files for 5.15.202 (vermagic 5.15.202 SMP preempt mod_unload modversions
aarch64). Its ip_set.ko imports resolve on the target device, and the symbols its
set types and xt_set.ko expect from the core are exactly the ones this core exports.
